"Your phone makes a loud sound. A few seconds later, the lamp begins to swing and the floor moves under your feet. If you live in Taiwan, you know this feeling well. Taiwan records about 40,000 earthquakes every year. Most are too small to notice, but people can feel about 1,000 of them. Why does the ground here shake so often? The answer begins with the Earth's outer shell. It is not one piece. It is broken into huge slabs of rock called plates, and the plates move very slowly, a few centimeters a year. Taiwan sits right where two plates meet: the Eurasian Plate and the Philippine Sea Plate. The Philippine Sea Plate pushes toward the northwest at about 7 to 8 centimeters a year. If the plates slid past each other smoothly, we would feel nothing. But rock is rough, and the plates get stuck along cracks called faults. The plate keeps pushing while the fault stays stuck, so the rock bends, a little like a plastic ruler. All that time, it is storing up force. At last the rock can hold no more. The fault slips suddenly, and the rock springs back. That is an earthquake. The slip sends out waves through the ground. The fastest ones, called P waves, arrive first and usually shake gently. The S waves come next, and they shake harder. Instruments near the earthquake catch the first waves, and computers quickly send an alert. For places far enough away, the alert can arrive seconds, or even tens of seconds, before the strong shaking. Most of Taiwan's earthquakes happen in the east, where the two plates meet. Yet earthquakes in the west often do more damage. They tend to be shallow, they happen under the land, and many people live there. A strong earthquake under a crowded plain is more dangerous than a stronger one far out at sea. Can scientists tell us when the next big one will come? Not yet. No one can predict the time, place, and size of an earthquake. What we can do is be ready. When the ground shakes, drop down, take cover under a strong table, and hold on. The same push that shakes Taiwan also built its high mountains. Earthquakes are part of living on a young, growing island.": "52ef8b5115fe",
